// VoxelMarchBound.usf -- THE BOUND PRODUCER (voxel.March.Bound, Stage 0b).
//
// Two passes, both added by VoxelMarchBoundProduce (VoxelMarchBound.cpp) only
// when the arm is on, immediately before the march pass:
//
//   VoxelMarchBoundListMain   [cs]  one thread per pool CHUNK SLOT over
//                                   VoxelBrickChunkTable -> per-level slot
//                                   lists + per-level FRHIDrawIndirectParameters
//   VoxelMarchBoundVS/PS      [raster, indirect, one draw per ring level]
//                                   one cube per listed slot into slice L of a
//                                   Texture2DArray<float2>, BO_Min both
//                                   channels, storing (tNear, -tFar)
//
// ===========================================================================
// WHY THE PRODUCER READS THE CHUNK TABLE AND NOTHING ELSE
// ===========================================================================
//
// VoxelBrickChunkTable is THE SAME TABLE the marcher validates every hit
// against (VoxelMarchLookupChunk's origin/level check reads dwords 0-3 of the
// same records). Building the bound from any CPU shadow of the resident set
// would be a JOIN COMPUTED INSTEAD OF CHECKED -- the derived-not-verified
// shape that produced five bugs in three days -- because the shadow and the
// table can disagree for exactly the frames a bound matters most (mid-flush).
// Reading the table means the bound and the hit test share one authority by
// construction.
//
// THE LIST PASS FILTERS ON LEVEL DECODE AND (SINCE 2026-08-28) THE VIEW
// FRUSTUM, AND NOTHING ELSE. No anySolid test, no index-points-at-this-slot
// join, no origin sanity test. A SUPERSET IS ALWAYS SAFE -- a stale or
// all-air cube only rasterises a slightly wider bound (lost benefit) --
// while any filter's bug fails in the direction of a missing cube, and a
// missing cube is a hole. The two admitted filters each carry a written
// no-hole argument: a slot decoding past the ring count (the cover level,
// 7) has no slice and no walk that would consult it, and a cube outside
// the padded ray frustum can be intersected by no marched ray at all --
// the argument, and every conservative choice it forced, is at the test
// in VoxelMarchBoundListMain. Any THIRD filter proposed here must bring
// the same shape of proof or stay out.
//
// ===========================================================================
// WHY THE PS COMPUTES t ANALYTICALLY, AND FROM WHICH RAY
// ===========================================================================
//
// The PS calls VoxelMarchBuildRay(SV_Position.xy + MarchViewRectMin) -- the
// EXACT function the march kernel runs (VoxelMarch.usf:866, pulled in by the
// #include below so there is one spelling in the binary, not a mirrored one;
// "the instrument must run the engine's binding"). Interpolated raster depth
// would be wrong twice over: it is the depth of a FACE (not the interval of
// the box), and it is quantised through the viewport transform. BuildRay also
// subtracts MarchTemporalAAJitter before constructing the direction -- a
// bound built for the unjittered ray would be a bound for a DIFFERENT ray
// than the one marched, off by the sub-pixel phase on exactly the frames TSR
// scrutinises hardest. And the analytic slab test hands the camera-inside-box
// case tNear <= 0 for free, with no near-plane special case.
//
// THE VS PROJECTS THROUGH THE SAME RAY MODEL, INVERTED. Coverage is what
// makes rasterisation sound here: a pixel-centre ray that hits INSIDE a cube
// projects strictly inside that cube's convex screen footprint, so the
// rasteriser covers the pixel (top-left rules only shave the measure-zero
// boundary). That argument only holds if the projection is the inverse of
// VoxelMarchBuildRay -- so the VS builds clip position as
//     clip.xy = ViewPos.xy / MarchInvProjDiag + MarchTemporalAAJitter * ViewPos.z
//     clip.z  = 0,  clip.w = ViewPos.z
// (division-free, so behind-the-camera vertices stay finite and the w=0 clip
// plane does the near clipping; z = 0 satisfies 0 <= z <= w for every w > 0).
// Dividing by w gives Ndc = ViewPos.xy / (z * InvProjDiag) + Jitter, and
// BuildRay's (Ndc - Jitter) * InvProjDiag recovers ViewPos.xy / z exactly:
// the pixel is covered if and only if THE MARCHED RAY meets the cube, up to
// float error the consumer's one-chunk BoundBiasUU absorbs in t (the lateral
// direction is covered by the strict-interior argument above). ViewPos itself
// comes from the transpose of MarchViewToTranslatedWorld's 3x3 -- that matrix
// is GetInvTranslatedViewMatrix (VoxelMarchRenderer.cpp:7120), rotation +
// translation with an orthonormal 3x3, and BuildRay uses it w=0, so the
// transpose IS the inverse rotation and mul(M, v) is the row-vector transpose
// trick.
//
// CULLING IS OFF AND DEPTH IS OFF. Back faces must rasterise (camera inside a
// cube sees only back faces; every exit point of every ray lies on one), and
// the interval written is a property of the BOX carried per-instance, not of
// the fragment's own face, so double-covered pixels write the same value
// twice and BO_Min makes that idempotent.

// The whole march shader, for VoxelMarchBuildRay and the uniforms it reads
// (MarchViewToTranslatedWorld, MarchRayOriginLocalUU, MarchInvProjDiag,
// MarchTemporalAAJitter, MarchViewRectMin/Size, MarchVolumeExtentUU) plus the
// pool globals VoxelBrickTraverse.ush declares (VoxelBrickChunkTable,
// VoxelBrickChunkSlots, VoxelBrickChunkRecordDwords, MarchBrickOriginVoxel).
// Including the .usf rather than restating BuildRay is deliberate: a second
// spelling of the ray is how two instruments end up describing two
// populations, and every unreferenced entry point below it is dead-stripped.
// The host pushes VOXEL_MARCH_SOURCE=1 (for the pool globals) and
// VOXEL_MARCH_RINGS=1 (so BuildRay compiles the CENTRED box the ring marcher
// runs -- the cornered variant is a different ray clip); VOXEL_MARCH_BOUND
// stays 0 here, so the CONSUMER half of VoxelMarchBound.ush does not compile
// into the producer and no bound texture global exists to bind.
#include "/VoxelEarth/VoxelMarch.usf"
#include "/VoxelEarth/VoxelMarchBound.ush"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Producer-only bindings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// How many ring levels get a slice this frame (== MarchRingCount as the host
// resolved it; passed separately so this file has no read-order dependency on
// the pool bind).
uint MarchBoundSlices;
// The per-level slot lists, SLICE-STRIDED BY THE POOL'S CHUNK CAPACITY:
// entry (L * VoxelBrickChunkSlots + i) is the i-th slot listed at level L. A
// slot lives in at most one level, so the flat allocation is Slices x
// capacity and no prefix sum is needed -- Stage 0b buys simplicity with a few
// transient megabytes (7 x ~131K x 4 B ~= 3.7 MB) rather than a second pass.
RWBuffer<uint> MarchBoundOutList;
Buffer<uint> MarchBoundList;
// One FRHIDrawIndirectParameters per level: [L*4+0] VertexCountPerInstance
// (36), [L*4+1] InstanceCount (atomic), [L*4+2] StartVertexLocation (0),
// [L*4+3] StartInstanceLocation (0). Cleared to zero by the host; field 0 is
// written by the first MarchBoundSlices threads of the list pass -- different
// dwords than the atomics, so no race.
RWBuffer<uint> MarchBoundOutArgs;
// Which level THIS indirect draw renders. A per-draw uniform, not an
// instance attribute: StartInstanceLocation cannot re-base SV_InstanceID, so
// the draw indexes its own stripe of the list with this.
uint MarchBoundLevel;

// ---- THE FRUSTUM CULL (added 2026-08-28: BND-eng2 measured the producer at
// boundMs 5.520 against marchMs 4.19 -- it cost more than the marcher it
// exists to relieve, and the dominant term was every live pool slot drawn
// with no visibility test).
//
// Five planes through the camera, in the LOCAL UU frame: the four sides of
// the ray pyramid plus the forward plane. DERIVED ON THE CPU FROM THE SAME
// MarchView QUANTITIES BuildRay reads -- corner rays built with the
// identical (Ndc - MarchTemporalAAJitter) * MarchInvProjDiag construction
// and the identical row-vector transform -- one authority, no drift -- and
// PADDED ONE FULL PIXEL OUTWARD on every side before the planes are taken,
// so both the jittered ray set and float slop live strictly inside the kept
// volume. xyz = inward unit normal, w = -dot(n, camera): inside is >= 0.
float4 MarchBoundCullPlane[5];
// 0 disables the test outright (the CPU found a degenerate plane -- NaN or
// a near-zero normal). FAIL-OPEN: disabled keeps every cube, i.e. the exact
// pre-cull behaviour; never a cull by garbage arithmetic.
uint MarchBoundCullEnable;
// The cull's own engagement words:
//   [0] considered  -- slots that decoded to a sliced level (pre-cull)
//   [1] culled      -- slots the plane test rejected
//   [2] enable      -- mirror of MarchBoundCullEnable, written by thread 0,
//                      so the CPU line can word culled==0 as DISABLED rather
//                      than as "nothing lay outside the frustum" (an arm
//                      that cannot show it fired is not an arm).
// Drawn-per-level is NOT duplicated here: it already exists as the indirect
// args' InstanceCount, read back beside this buffer from the SAME dispatch,
// and considered == culled + sum(drawn) is the identity the log line checks.
RWBuffer<uint> MarchBoundOutCullStats;

// ---- THE HALF-RES DILATION SLOPE (same leg, second mitigation). The bound
// target is now HALF RESOLUTION PER AXIS (a quarter of the fill) and the
// conservatism moved into the PRODUCER as geometry: each cube is inflated,
// before projection AND before the PS slab test, by
//     R = MarchBoundDilateSlope * (distance to the cube's farthest point).
// Unitless slope (UU of inflation per UU of distance), set by the host to
// 4x MarchPixelConeSlope (the full-res pixel HALF-width slope). Why 4x is
// enough is argued at the VS; the producer DECLINES rather than running
// with a non-positive slope, because R = 0 at half res is not conservative.
float MarchBoundDilateSlope;

#ifndef VOXEL_MARCH_BOUND_LIST_GROUP
#define VOXEL_MARCH_BOUND_LIST_GROUP 64
#endif

// The chunk record's cube in the local UU frame -- ONE SPELLING, shared by
// the list pass's cull and the VS's projection. Two derivations of "where
// is this cube" is how a cull comes to reject a cube the raster would have
// drawn, and in this file's failure algebra that is a hole, not an
// artefact. The arithmetic is the one VoxelMarchBeginLevel sets up:
// RecOrigin is stored in level-L voxels, the frame origin shifts exactly
// (the 1024-voxel snap), and a chunk is 32 level-L voxels.
void VoxelMarchBoundChunkBox(uint Slot, uint Level, out float3 OutBoxMinUU,
                             out float3 OutBoxMaxUU)
{
	const uint Base = Slot * VoxelBrickChunkRecordDwords;
	const int3 RecOrigin = int3(int(VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 0u]),
	                            int(VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 1u]),
	                            int(VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 2u]));
	const float VoxUU = VOXEL_FLUID_VOXEL_UU * float(1u << Level);
	const int3 OriginL = int3(MarchBrickOriginVoxel.x >> Level,
	                          MarchBrickOriginVoxel.y >> Level,
	                          MarchBrickOriginVoxel.z >> Level);
	OutBoxMinUU = float3(RecOrigin - OriginL) * VoxUU;
	OutBoxMaxUU = OutBoxMinUU + 32.0f * VoxUU;
}

// ===========================================================================
// 1. THE LIST PASS -- pattern: VoxelMarchCompactTilesMain
// ===========================================================================
[numthreads(VOXEL_MARCH_BOUND_LIST_GROUP, 1, 1)]
void VoxelMarchBoundListMain(uint3 DispatchThreadId : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint SlotIndex = DispatchThreadId.x;

	// The non-atomic indirect-args fields, one thread per level. Placed before
	// the slot guard so a dispatch sized for a tiny pool still initialises
	// every level's vertex count.
	if (SlotIndex < MarchBoundSlices)
	{
		MarchBoundOutArgs[SlotIndex * 4u + 0u] = 36u; // VertexCountPerInstance: 12 tris
		MarchBoundOutArgs[SlotIndex * 4u + 2u] = 0u;  // StartVertexLocation
		MarchBoundOutArgs[SlotIndex * 4u + 3u] = 0u;  // StartInstanceLocation
		// [L*4+1] InstanceCount stays at the host's clear (0) and is grown by
		// the atomics below.
	}
	if (SlotIndex == 0u)
	{
		// The enable mirror -- see the declaration. Written whether or not
		// the cull is on, so the CPU never reads the buffer's clear as a
		// state the shader chose.
		MarchBoundOutCullStats[2] = MarchBoundCullEnable;
	}

	if (SlotIndex >= VoxelBrickChunkSlots)
	{
		return;
	}

	// Dword 3 of the record is LevelAndFlags; bits [0:3] are the ring level --
	// the SAME decode VoxelMarchLookupChunk validates hits against ("the level
	// MASKS to four bits (it does not clamp) -- confirmed against
	// BuildChunkRecord, which is the writer").
	const uint Base = SlotIndex * VoxelBrickChunkRecordDwords;
	const uint LevelAndFlags = VoxelBrickChunkTable[Base + 3u];
	const uint Level = LevelAndFlags & 0xFu;

	// EVERY slot that decodes at a sliced level is included -- no anySolid
	// filter, no index join, no origin sanity test. Superset doctrine: see the
	// header. A free or stale slot rasterises a harmless extra cube; a filter
	// bug would delete a real one, and that failure direction is a hole.
	if (Level >= MarchBoundSlices)
	{
		return;
	}

	InterlockedAdd(MarchBoundOutCullStats[0], 1u); // considered (pre-cull)

	// ===================================================================
	// THE FRUSTUM CULL, AND ITS ONE-WAY FAILURE ALGEBRA
	// ===================================================================
	//
	// A wrongly-DRAWN cube costs microseconds of VS and fill. A wrongly-
	// CULLED cube is missing from the hull, and a missing contribution
	// shrinks tFar -- WalkOut then clamps short of real terrain and the
	// walk never reaches it: A HOLE, this project's report-on-sight
	// defect. Every choice below therefore leans KEEP:
	//   * the planes come from the JITTERED corner rays (the same
	//     (Ndc - MarchTemporalAAJitter) subtraction BuildRay applies),
	//     padded ONE FULL PIXEL outward on every side, so no ray the march
	//     can build this frame exits the kept volume;
	//   * the test is the box's POSITIVE VERTEX (the corner farthest along
	//     each plane normal): it rejects only boxes whose NEAREST approach
	//     is strictly outside a padded plane;
	//   * touch and ambiguity KEEP (`< 0` strict, and a NaN comparison
	//     reads false, which lands on the keep side);
	//   * a degenerate frustum disables the cull outright
	//     (MarchBoundCullEnable 0) rather than culling with garbage.
	//
	// SOUNDNESS: a chunk any marched ray HITS contains the hit point, so
	// it intersects that ray, and that ray lies inside the unpadded
	// pyramid -- the padded positive-vertex test cannot reject it. The
	// cubes this removes can contribute to no hit's covering interval;
	// dropping them narrows the hull toward (never past) the resident
	// extent, which is lost benefit's direction, not a hole's.
	//
	// ON THE UNINFLATED BOX, DELIBERATELY: the VS's half-res dilation
	// exists for texel-footprint coverage, and the hit-coverage argument
	// above never needed the inflation -- a cube whose inflation alone
	// touches the frustum covers no real hit.
	if (MarchBoundCullEnable != 0u)
	{
		float3 CullBoxMinUU;
		float3 CullBoxMaxUU;
		VoxelMarchBoundChunkBox(SlotIndex, Level, CullBoxMinUU, CullBoxMaxUU);
		[unroll]
		for (int PlaneIdx = 0; PlaneIdx < 5; ++PlaneIdx)
		{
			const float4 Pl = MarchBoundCullPlane[PlaneIdx];
			const float3 PositiveVertex =
				float3(Pl.x >= 0.0f ? CullBoxMaxUU.x : CullBoxMinUU.x,
				       Pl.y >= 0.0f ? CullBoxMaxUU.y : CullBoxMinUU.y,
				       Pl.z >= 0.0f ? CullBoxMaxUU.z : CullBoxMinUU.z);
			if (dot(Pl.xyz, PositiveVertex) + Pl.w < 0.0f)
			{
				InterlockedAdd(MarchBoundOutCullStats[1], 1u); // culled
				return;
			}
		}
	}

	uint WriteIndex = 0u;
	InterlockedAdd(MarchBoundOutArgs[Level * 4u + 1u], 1u, WriteIndex);
	MarchBoundOutList[Level * VoxelBrickChunkSlots + WriteIndex] = SlotIndex;
}

// ===========================================================================
// 2. THE RASTER PASS -- pattern: the DepthPreEmit draw (GEmptyVertexDeclaration,
//    corners from SV_VertexID, one indirect instanced draw per level)
// ===========================================================================

struct FVoxelMarchBoundInterp
{
	float4 Position : SV_Position;
	// nointerpolation: the interval is a property of the BOX, identical at
	// every vertex of the instance, and interpolating it would only invite a
	// precision wobble the PS then bakes into a jump start.
	nointerpolation float3 BoxMinUU : TEXCOORD0;
	nointerpolation float3 BoxMaxUU : TEXCOORD1;
};

// 12 triangles over the 8 corners; corner c decodes to
// (c & 1, (c >> 1) & 1, (c >> 2) & 1). Winding is irrelevant: CM_None.
static const uint kVoxelMarchBoundCubeIndex[36] =
{
	0u, 4u, 6u,  0u, 6u, 2u,   // -X
	1u, 3u, 7u,  1u, 7u, 5u,   // +X
	0u, 1u, 5u,  0u, 5u, 4u,   // -Y
	2u, 6u, 7u,  2u, 7u, 3u,   // +Y
	0u, 2u, 3u,  0u, 3u, 1u,   // -Z
	4u, 5u, 7u,  4u, 7u, 6u    // +Z
};

FVoxelMarchBoundInterp VoxelMarchBoundVS(uint VertexId : SV_VertexID,
                                         uint InstanceId : SV_InstanceID)
{
	FVoxelMarchBoundInterp Out = (FVoxelMarchBoundInterp)0;

	const uint Slot = MarchBoundList[MarchBoundLevel * VoxelBrickChunkSlots + InstanceId];
	float3 BoxMinUU;
	float3 BoxMaxUU;
	VoxelMarchBoundChunkBox(Slot, MarchBoundLevel, BoxMinUU, BoxMaxUU);

	// ===================================================================
	// THE HALF-RES DILATION, AND WHY IT IS CONSERVATIVE
	// ===================================================================
	//
	// The target is half resolution per axis, so one texel now stands for
	// the 2x2 block of full-res rays the consumer will ask it about, while
	// the PS computes its interval for ONE ray -- the block-centre ray.
	// Two gaps open, and one inflation closes both (the same farthest-of-
	// four conservatism the half-res VisBuffer reconstruction argues at
	// VoxelMarch.usf's depth-read note, moved into the producer where it
	// is paid once per CUBE instead of four loads per RAY per level):
	//
	//   COVERAGE: a full-res ray can hit a cube whose silhouette misses
	//   the texel centre, so the unwritten texel would read EMPTY over a
	//   real hit -- a hole. Inflating the cube by more than the block's
	//   angular radius forces its footprint over the texel centre for
	//   every cube any of the block's rays can hit.
	//
	//   THE t VALUES: the stored interval is the centre ray's. For any
	//   ray q in the block and any point x of the TRUE cube on q, the
	//   centre ray passes within (block angular radius) x t of x, so the
	//   centre ray's point at the same t lies inside the INFLATED cube --
	//   the stored [tNear, tFar] of the inflated cube brackets every hit
	//   t of every ray in the block.
	//
	// THE RADIUS: worst offset between a block ray and the texel-centre
	// ray is 0.5 full-res px in each axis = 0.7071 px, and the half-res
	// raster grid's NDC skew on an odd-sized rect misplaces the centre by
	// up to ~1 px more; 1.71 px = 3.41 pixel HALF-widths. The host sets
	// MarchBoundDilateSlope = 4 x MarchPixelConeSlope (the half-width
	// slope), a ~17% margin on the worst case, and inflation only ever
	// WIDENS the stored interval -- the failure direction of a too-large
	// R is lost benefit, never a hole. TAA jitter needs no extra term:
	// producer and consumer rays subtract the SAME MarchTemporalAAJitter,
	// so their relative offsets are jitter-free.
	//
	// Evaluated at the cube's FARTHEST point from the camera, so R covers
	// the largest t at which this cube can be hit.
	{
		const float3 BoxCentre = 0.5f * (BoxMinUU + BoxMaxUU);
		const float FarDistUU =
			length(BoxCentre - MarchRayOriginLocalUU) + length(BoxMaxUU - BoxCentre);
		const float R = MarchBoundDilateSlope * FarDistUU;
		BoxMinUU -= R;
		BoxMaxUU += R;
	}
	Out.BoxMinUU = BoxMinUU;
	Out.BoxMaxUU = BoxMaxUU;

	const uint Corner = kVoxelMarchBoundCubeIndex[VertexId];
	const float3 CornerUU = float3((Corner & 1u) != 0u ? BoxMaxUU.x : BoxMinUU.x,
	                               (Corner & 2u) != 0u ? BoxMaxUU.y : BoxMinUU.y,
	                               (Corner & 4u) != 0u ? BoxMaxUU.z : BoxMinUU.z);

	// Ray-consistent projection -- see the header. LocalDelta is the corner
	// relative to the CAMERA in the local frame (MarchRayOriginLocalUU is the
	// camera: it is what BuildRay uses as every ray's origin).
	const float3 LocalDelta = CornerUU - MarchRayOriginLocalUU;
	// Inverse of BuildRay's `mul(float4(DirView, 0), MarchViewToTranslatedWorld)`:
	// the 3x3 is orthonormal (GetInvTranslatedViewMatrix), so the transpose is
	// the inverse, and mul(M, v) applies the transpose of the row-vector form.
	const float3 ViewPos = mul(MarchViewToTranslatedWorld, float4(LocalDelta, 0.0f)).xyz;

	// Division-free homogeneous position: xy pre-multiplied by w = ViewPos.z,
	// z = 0 so 0 <= z <= w clips exactly at the camera plane and nowhere else
	// (the analytic PS neither needs nor wants a far clip).
	Out.Position = float4(ViewPos.xy / MarchInvProjDiag + MarchTemporalAAJitter * ViewPos.z,
	                      0.0f, ViewPos.z);
	return Out;
}

void VoxelMarchBoundPS(FVoxelMarchBoundInterp In, out float2 OutInterval : SV_Target0)
{
	// HALF-RES TARGET, FULL-RES RAY MODEL. Texel (tx, ty) stands for the
	// 2x2 full-res block whose shared centre sits at full-res position
	// (2tx + 1, 2ty + 1) = 2 * In.Position.xy (the rasteriser hands texel
	// centres, t + 0.5). BuildRay is then the march's own, unmodified --
	// MarchViewRectSize stays the REAL rect, because feeding it the padded
	// half-res extent would silently change the camera model (a different
	// FOV per pixel), which is a different ray, which is no bound at all.
	// The block's other rays are covered by the VS dilation, not by this
	// mapping.
	const FVoxelMarchRay Ray = VoxelMarchBuildRay(MarchViewRectMin + 2.0f * In.Position.xy);

	// The slab test, in the same SafeDir discipline BuildRay's box clip uses
	// (an exact-zero component nudged rather than divided).
	const float3 SafeDir =
		Ray.DirWorld + float3(abs(Ray.DirWorld) < VOXEL_MARCH_MIN_DIR_COMPONENT) *
		                   VOXEL_MARCH_MIN_DIR_COMPONENT;
	const float3 InvDir = 1.0f / SafeDir;
	const float3 T0 = (In.BoxMinUU - Ray.OriginLocalUU) * InvDir;
	const float3 T1 = (In.BoxMaxUU - Ray.OriginLocalUU) * InvDir;
	const float3 TLo = min(T0, T1);
	const float3 THi = max(T0, T1);
	// max(..., 0): behind-the-camera geometry cannot be walked, and a negative
	// near would only widen the hull below t=0 for nothing. Camera inside the
	// box lands here as TNear = 0 exactly -- the free case the header names.
	const float TNear = max(max(TLo.x, TLo.y), max(TLo.z, 0.0f));
	const float TFar = min(min(THi.x, THi.y), THi.z);

	if (TFar < TNear)
	{
		// A covered pixel whose analytic ray misses the box: possible on the
		// footprint boundary, where coverage (a rounded rasteriser decision)
		// and the exact slab test disagree by a ULP. Write the identity of
		// BO_Min -- +BIG in both channels -- so this fragment narrows nothing
		// and empties nothing.
		OutInterval = float2(VOXEL_MARCH_BOUND_EMPTY, VOXEL_MARCH_BOUND_EMPTY);
		return;
	}
	OutInterval = VoxelMarchBoundEncode(TNear, TFar);
}
